From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: this console has POSIX semaphore headers and no POSIX semaphores

The core loaded, ran, and died with two uncaught exceptions:

    [orbis] libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type
            std::__1::system_error: sem_wait: Invalid argument
    [orbis] libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type
            std::__1::system_error: sem_post: Invalid argument

src/libretro/std/semaphore.hpp picks a counting_semaphore per platform, and this SDK answers
to __unix__, so it took yamc::posix::counting_semaphore - confirmed in the linked module,
which carries yamc::posix::counting_semaphore<2147483647>::acquire() and ::release(). That
class calls ::sem_init, ::sem_wait and ::sem_post directly.

⚠ THE SDK'S sem_* ARE FreeBSD KERNEL SEMAPHORES, MEASURED IN THE LINKED CORE RATHER THAN READ
OUT OF THE HEADERS:

    sem_post:      jmp   ksem_post@plt
    sem_trywait:   jmp   ksem_trywait@plt
    sem_wait:      xorl  %esi, %esi ; jmp sem_timedwait -> ksem_timedwait@plt
    sem_init:      testl %esi, %esi          ; pshared
                   jne   -> errno = 1 (EPERM), return -1
                   movl  %edx, %esi ; jmp ksem_init@plt

Those ksem_* entry points do not work for this process, and the header describes something the
kernel implements differently - the same shape of defect as the SYS_gettid aliasing that
ps4/orbis_cxa_guard.c corrects.

⚠ AND THE CONSTRUCTOR HIDES IT, WHICH IS WHY TWO LATER CALLS FAIL AND NOT THE CREATION.
posix_semaphore.hpp:

    ::sem_init(&sem_, 0, (unsigned int)desired);
    // counting_semaphore constructor throws nothing.

The return value is discarded. sem_ keeps whatever was in that heap allocation, so every
acquire and release afterwards hands the kernel an id that was never valid, gets EINVAL, and
throws - from two different call sites, with nothing pointing at the initialization that
actually failed.

⚠ THIS IS THE FOURTH ARM OF A FILE WHOSE ENTIRE JOB IS CHOOSING ONE, not a new primitive.
yamc already ships a portable counting_semaphore over std::mutex and std::condition_variable,
it implements every member melonDS DS uses - acquire, release, try_acquire, try_acquire_for -
and this port has already made both primitives work (ps4/orbis_cv_fix.cpp replaces
std::condition_variable's members in libc++.a). Interposing the POSIX semaphore family instead
would be a platform primitive to maintain for one vendored dependency: nothing else here uses
it - RetroArch's own ssem_* is mutex-and-condvar in rthreads.c, and the frontend does not
reference sem_init at all.

⚠ AND IT GOES FIRST, ahead of __cpp_lib_semaphore. If libc++ ever reports <semaphore> here its
__libcpp_semaphore_* are POSIX sem_t on this platform too, and would fail in the same way.

diff --git a/src/libretro/std/semaphore.hpp b/src/libretro/std/semaphore.hpp
index 839dd2d..4c4f888 100644
--- a/src/libretro/std/semaphore.hpp
+++ b/src/libretro/std/semaphore.hpp
@@ -17,7 +17,54 @@
 #ifndef MELONDS_DS_STD_SEMAPHORE_HPP
 #define MELONDS_DS_STD_SEMAPHORE_HPP
 
-#ifdef __cpp_lib_semaphore
+/* ⚠ THE PLAYSTATION 4 HAS POSIX SEMAPHORE HEADERS AND NO WORKING POSIX SEMAPHORES, and this
+ * arm goes FIRST because both branches below it would walk into that.
+ *
+ * This file already picks an implementation per platform; the __unix__ arm takes
+ * yamc::posix::counting_semaphore, which calls ::sem_init / ::sem_wait / ::sem_post directly.
+ * On a console that ends the core:
+ *
+ *     [orbis] libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type
+ *             std::__1::system_error: sem_wait: Invalid argument
+ *     [orbis] libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type
+ *             std::__1::system_error: sem_post: Invalid argument
+ *
+ * The SDK's sem_* are thin wrappers over FreeBSD's kernel semaphore syscalls - measured in a
+ * linked core, sem_post is `jmp ksem_post@plt`, sem_wait tail-calls sem_timedwait into
+ * ksem_timedwait, and sem_init(sem, pshared, value) refuses a non-zero pshared with EPERM and
+ * otherwise tail-calls ksem_init. Those ksem_* entry points do not work for this process.
+ *
+ * ⚠ AND THE FAILURE IS SILENT UNTIL MUCH LATER, WHICH IS WHY BOTH LATER CALLS FAIL AND NOT THE
+ * CONSTRUCTOR. posix_semaphore.hpp's constructor is
+ *
+ *     ::sem_init(&sem_, 0, (unsigned int)desired);
+ *     // counting_semaphore constructor throws nothing.
+ *
+ * - the return value is DISCARDED. So sem_ keeps whatever was in that heap allocation, and
+ * every acquire and release afterwards hands the kernel a semaphore id that was never valid
+ * and gets EINVAL, from two different call sites, with nothing to say the initialization was
+ * the thing that failed.
+ *
+ * ⚠ SO THIS IS NOT A WORKAROUND AND NOT A NEW PLATFORM PRIMITIVE - IT IS THE FOURTH ARM OF A
+ * FILE WHOSE ENTIRE JOB IS CHOOSING ONE. yamc ships a portable counting_semaphore built on
+ * std::mutex and std::condition_variable for exactly this case, it implements every member
+ * melonDS DS uses (acquire, release, try_acquire, try_acquire_for), and this port has already
+ * made both of those primitives work - see ps4/orbis_cv_fix.cpp in the RetroArch tree, which
+ * replaces std::condition_variable's members in libc++.a. Nothing else in this port uses POSIX
+ * semaphores: RetroArch's own ssem_* is mutex-and-condvar in rthreads.c and the frontend does
+ * not reference sem_init at all, so there is nothing here worth interposing a whole POSIX
+ * semaphore family for.
+ *
+ * ⚠ FIRST, NOT AFTER __cpp_lib_semaphore. If libc++ ever reports <semaphore>, its
+ * __libcpp_semaphore_* are POSIX sem_t on this platform too and would fail the same way. */
+#if defined(__ORBIS__)
+#include <yamc_semaphore.hpp>
+
+namespace std {
+    template <std::ptrdiff_t least_max_value = YAMC_SEMAPHORE_LEAST_MAX_VALUE>
+    using counting_semaphore = yamc::counting_semaphore<least_max_value>;
+}
+#elif defined(__cpp_lib_semaphore)
 #include <semaphore>
 #elif defined(WIN32)
 #include <win_semaphore.hpp>
